hi this is pressure Locker in this video  I'm going to explain the 80/20 rule  which is also known as the Pareto  principle the 80/20 rule states that 80%  of the results come from 20% of the  causes it was observed in 1906 by the  economist vilfredo pareto who noticed  that 80% of the land in Italy was owned  by only 20% of the population the 80/20  rule has many examples for instance 80%  of the world income comes from 20% of  the richest people the 80/20 rule is a  good rule of thumb so that you can focus  on only 20% of the causes to improve 80%  of the results but why does it happen  let's do an experiment to find out we'll  start our experiment with a hundred  paper clips well mix them into a pile  and select two paper clips at random  well join those paper clips into a chain  and then we'll put them back into the  pile we'll repeat the experiment by  selecting two paper clips at random and  joining them into a chain we'll repeat  this experiment over and over again each  time joining paper clips into chains if  we selected them randomly over time  there will be some chains of paperclips  which get very long in fact chains which  are longer will be more likely to get  picked and they'll form even longer  chains after we repeat this experiment  over and over again we can look at the  distribution of paperclip chains because  the longer chains are more likely to get  picked this is an example of how the  rich can get richer in our distribution  of paperclip chains there will be many  paper clips that are single and by  themselves and a few paper clips which  are very long chains and this is where  the 80/20 rule can be Illustrated 80% of  the paperclips belong to the top 20% of  the longest chains in fact if we were to  graph out the data we would find what's  known as a power law where there are a  lot of paper clips that of a very small  size but there are a few paper clips  that have very high valued size and take  up a majority of the  paperclips this type of graph is also  seen with income distribution now we  know why the 80/20 rule happens it's an  emergent phenomenon from a process where  ever the rich get richer the 80/20 rule  is also a pretty good rule of thumb for  efficiency I hope you like this video  please subscribe to my channel I make  videos on math and game theory you can  support mind your decisions on patreon  and get exclusive rewards you can catch  me on my blog mind your decisions on  Twitter at pressure Locker or get my  books at Amazon
 

